  "narrative": "The differential metabolites are strongly enriched in **Eicosanoid Synthesis** (WikiPathways WP167, FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 1,251×), with five of the eight input compounds—arachidonic acid, 15-Deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ₂, 5-HETE, 15-HETE, and leukotriene A4—confirmed as pathway members. A second, tightly associated SMPDB entry, **Leukotriene C4 Synthesis Deficiency** (FDR = 0.0, fold-enrichment = 536×), shares the same five driver metabolites, reinforcing the conclusion that this is the primary affected pathway.\n\n**Key drivers.** Arachidonic acid (C00219) is the canonical upstream precursor. The KEGG reaction graph confirms it is connected bidirectionally to Leukotriene A4 (C00909) through an intermediate (C05356) in just two hops, indicating they are co-produced metabolites of the same oxidative cascade. 15-Deoxy-Δ¹²,¹⁴-PGJ₂ (C14717), 5-HETE (C04805), and 15-HETE (C04742) each represent distinct downstream arms—cyclooxygenase-derived prostaglandin signaling and lipoxygenase-derived hydroxyeicosatetraenoic acid signaling—branching from the same arachidonic acid pool. Pathway membership for arachidonic acid and 5-HETE was independently confirmed against the SMPDB Arachidonic Acid Metabolism reference.\n\n**Biological significance.** Coordinated elevation of these five eicosanoids implicates intensified phospholipase-A₂-mediated arachidonic acid mobilization, followed by parallel activation of COX, LOX-5, and LOX-12/15 enzymatic branches. 15d-PGJ₂ is a recognized endogenous electrophile that activates Nrf2-mediated antioxidant response, while HETEs and leukotrienes are potent lipid mediators of inflammation, leukocyte chemotaxis, and vascular tone. Acetylcysteine (elevated) may reflect compensatory glutathione synthesis to counteract electrophilic stress generated by 15d-PGJ₂. Dopamine and fructose-1-phosphate fall outside the eicosanoid cluster and likely represent secondary metabolic consequences of the primary inflammatory-lipid shift.\n\nIn summary, the treatment group is characterized by a broad activation of the arachidonic acid–eicosanoid axis, with arachidonic acid and leukotriene A4 confirmed as interconnected nodes in a tightly enriched inflammatory-lipid pathway.",
